this is a production of Cornell  University  okay good morning I'm Greg Peck and I  love monologist meaning I work with food  crops and particularly with orchard  systems and very specifically with apple  orchard systems and so we've been  thinking a lot about soil health for  orchard systems for a long time many  decades and it's been a big part of my  graduate research and in my professional  research since then but some of the  things that are quite different about  apple orchards and vegetable systems or  about row crop systems and agronomic  crops is that we only get into the soil  about once every 20 to 30 years to do  tillage practices that's our opportunity  to cover crops we do pre planned cover  cropping we might add some compost we  adjust pH then we plant the trees and  then the perennial system so those trees  are there for a very long time for  decades so then the question becomes  well from a soil health perspective what  can we do to increase the soil health of  the system orchards have very different  root structures than annual systems but  also we've seen a change in our orchard  systems over the past thirty years and  so that top picture shows what a  traditional orchard looked like thirty  years ago  widely spaced trees on a 25 by 25 foot  grid now we plant trees three feet apart  some girls are now even planting trees a  foot and a half apart and twelve feet  between rows so very intensive systems  they require a lot more precision in  terms of our nutrient use in terms of  our water use and potentially we have an  opportunity here to do things to the  soil to make those trees more productive  so the bottom picture there is showing  myself and former graduate student who's  no professor out at Oregon State with  some dwarfing rootstocks so we're using  these new genotypes that produce very  small trees and with that very small  root systems so one of the ways that  we've been looking at to increase soil  health and orchard systems is through  the use of mulch  and so whereas we can't use cover crops  since her defrosting a compost on an  annual basis we can mulch on and what  we're finding is is that while sometimes  tree growth and yield is is not always  improved although I'd have to say look  back at three of the last studies that  we did and we always found a increase in  tree growth particularly for our young  plantings by using mulch and where I  think we're seeing that more  particularly in years like two years ago  and again this year when we have it a  very dry June after planting but we're  seeing all these other benefits for soil  health that have been talked about  already this morning and many of you are  familiar with increasing soil organic  matter increasing the water holding  capacity increasing biological activity  making nutrients and minerals available  for the plants we see some suppressive  effects for diseases erosion control on  hillsides which is really important for  our Hudson River Valley growers that  grow on more slopes and on the picture  here this is actually a organically  managed orchard and we're finding that  we are able to control weeds effectively  with mulch with an overlay of a second  practice so if we do mulch with an  herbicide we with an organic herbicide  we can have a weed free strip that is  very similar to what the conventional  growers are getting with with Street  herbicide so this has always been the  barrier for organic it's always we  control true in in any cropping system  and it's true in an orchard systems so  it's really exciting we're finding tons  of benefits reduced leaching of  nutrients and pesticides into the soil  so mulch is really where it's at  other positive effects what this chart  is showing you is that under mulch which  for the bars the four bars on the left  side are the mulch treatments and the  bars on the right hand side are  different no mulch treatments with  without compost with without calcium  nitrate that mulching is increasing  mycorrhizae in the soil so these are  microorganisms that are helping getting  water and phosphorus into the plant and  we're finding great benefits from that  so things that growers me  realize are happening or happening below  the ground so with the soil health  project we really want our metrics we  want ways of trying to understand how do  we measure soil health for orchard  systems we may not always be able to  show it in short-term dollars and cents  but we can show it in long-term benefits  the soil long term benefits to the  environment and I think that's really  important for us as we think about soil  health is that we expand the crops that  we're looking at we're looking at  agronomic crops we're looking at  vegetable system and that we're also  looking at cranial fruit systems this is  another picture of just a trial looking  at integrating practices compost and  nutrients and other ways of thinking  about this creatively Ann Arbor is are  very interested in this I was just  invited on Thursday to talk to soil  health - about finding 50 commercial  apple growers along Lake Ontario so they  are asking these questions and these are  not organic just organic growers these  are our largest conventional mainstream  growers in the state are very interested  so thanks  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at Cornell edu
 

